            "A positioning matrix’s usefulness depends on being looked at, not merely computed, and reproducibility requires that its plotting conventions be made explicit rather than left to whichever convention a given figure-maker reaches for. Three conventions are specified here. First, both axes should be plotted on their full normalised 0–100 range rather than auto-scaled to the observed spread of whichever industries populate a given comparison set, since auto-scaling would visually exaggerate differences that are, on the underlying scale, modest. Second, quadrant boundary lines should be drawn as dashed lines or shaded bands rather than crisp solid lines — a deliberate departure from the conventional BCG- and GE–McKinsey-style presentation — because the axis scores are constructed composites whose underlying weights are not (yet) independently derived, and a crisp boundary visually implies a categorical precision the underlying measurement does not support. Third, following the GE–McKinsey convention of encoding a third variable as bubble size (GE & McKinsey & Company, n.d.), a SAIM 2.0 plot may encode workforce size as bubble size and composite resilience tier as bubble colour, subject to Cleveland and McGill’s (1985) finding that viewers judge position along a common scale more accurately than area, and Few’s (2012) and Tufte’s (2001) general cautions against overloading a static two-dimensional display with more visual channels than a reader can reliably disentangle.",
