        "This paper has developed SAIM 2.0, a two-axis, four-quadrant conceptual positioning matrix that classifies industries by AI Readiness and Workforce Vulnerability into four differentiated intervention quadrants, and has reported a systematic comparative framework analysis benchmarking that matrix, together with its companion composite index, against twenty-three existing frameworks and analytical tools on eight predefined evaluation criteria. Two things are worth restating plainly in closing. First, the comparison finds that no existing instrument located by this review jointly addresses readiness, vulnerability, adaptive capacity, and societal impact at industry level using a purpose-built two-axis matrix — a claim about the coverage of the existing published literature, checkable directly against the cited instruments, not a claim about any instrument’s real-world performance. Second, and just as important, the comparison’s own maximal score for the proposed instrument is a designed rather than an achieved property, produced by criteria the instrument’s own designer helped derive, and should be read as evidence of the instrument’s novelty relative to the reviewed literature rather than as evidence that it works. Comparative framework analysis, conducted with a stated-in-advance rubric, a documented inclusion boundary, and candid acknowledgement of its own circularity, is offered here as one legitimate and reusable literature-based validation method for conceptual and design-science instrument development in this fast-moving field — not as a substitute for the independent expert re-scoring, independently derived criteria, and empirical field-testing of quadrant placements that this paper identifies, in specific and operational terms, as the necessary next steps before SAIM 2.0 could reasonably be described as anything more than a proposed and conceptually validated tool."
